The {{Nihongo|'''Toyozawa Mainichi'''|豊沢毎日|Toyozawa Mainichi|lit. "Toyozawa every day"}}, stylized in Mercian as '''The Toyozawa Mainichi''', is a [[Sanese language|Sanese-language]] daily newspaper published in [[Toyozawa]], [[Okaiken]]. It is the largest newspaper in the Tokuyama Prefecture region and the third-largest nationally by morning edition circulation. The paper is considered a {{w|Newspaper of record|newspaper of record}} for Okaiken's industrial heartland.

The Toyozawa Mainichi is one of the five major national dailies of Okaiken, alongside the ''Okami Shimbun'', the ''Noto Keizai Shimbun'', the ''[[The Okami Times|Okami Times]]'', and the ''Sanese Sankei''. Its editorial line is generally centre-right, with a reputation for pragmatic conservatism and strong coverage of industrial policy, labour relations, and regional Tokuyama affairs.

==History==

===Founding and pre-war era (1935–1945)===
The Toyozawa Mainichi was founded on 18 April 1935 by Toyozawa industrialist {{Nihongo|Kudō Isamu|工藤勇|}} as a regional daily serving the rapidly industrialising Tokuyama Prefecture. The paper's founding editorial declared: "Industry builds the nation; the press must hold industry to account." From its inception, the Mainichi occupied a pro-business, pragmatic position — supportive of industrial development but critical of cronyism between government and zaibatsu.

During the militarist period, the paper was placed under censorship like all Sanese publications, but its regional focus and industrial readership allowed it to maintain a degree of independence. Kudō was briefly detained in 1943 for publishing production figures that contradicted government claims, an incident the paper commemorates as "Honest Numbers Day."

===Post-war expansion (1945–1970)===
Following the occupation, the Toyozawa Mainichi expanded rapidly alongside Toyozawa's industrial boom. The paper's coverage of the emerging labour movement was more sympathetic to management than its Okami-based competitors, but its industrial reporting was widely respected for its technical depth.

During the [[Quiet Revolution]] (1967–1968), the Mainichi took a cautious editorial line — critical of the Kusaka government's constitutional overreach but skeptical of the radical left's demands for structural reform. The paper's editorial on the 1968 riots, titled "The Centre Must Hold," argued that "revolution devours its children, but so does reaction."

===Modern era (1970–present)===
Throughout the 1970s and 1980s, the Toyozawa Mainichi established itself as the voice of the industrial establishment — pro-business, pro-stability, and deeply skeptical of both leftist radicalism and LDP corruption. Its coverage of the Miyazaki Hotel Scandal was more cautious than the Okami Times, favouring documented evidence over investigative speculation, but ultimately endorsed the call for accountability.

In 2023, the paper launched a Mercian Translation Edition, following the Okami Times into the international market. The move was initially resisted by traditionalists who argued it was "following Okami, not leading," but the edition has proven commercially viable, with digital readership tripling by 2026.

The paper's coverage of the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] was notably more sympathetic to the Honda government's technology policy than the Okami Times, arguing that "the failure was in implementation, not ambition." It endorsed the PPO in the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021]] and [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025]] elections, but unlike the Okami Times, it did not withdraw its endorsement for the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026]] election, citing "the need for stability over recrimination."

==Political stance==
The Toyozawa Mainichi is considered centre-right, with a long tradition of pro-business, pragmatic conservative commentary. It has been described as "the newspaper of the responsible right" — more willing to defend the establishment than the centrist ''Okami Times'', but more moderate than the nationalist ''Sanese Sankei''.

The paper supported the [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda]] government's technology investment and social programs, but was critical of what it called "regulatory overreach" in the Digital Sovereignty Act. It endorsed the PPO in the 2026 election, arguing that "the nation needs steady hands, not a lurch to the left or a return to the old guard."

The Toyozawa Mainichi has historically advocated for a strong national defence and supported the development of autonomous weapons systems for defensive purposes, positions that have drawn criticism from pacifist quarters.

==Sections==
The daily edition is organized into the following sections:
* '''Headlines''' (''Top News'') — Front-page political and national news
* '''Entertainment''' (''Culture & Arts'') — Culture, music, television, film, and literature
* '''International''' (''World'') — Foreign affairs and diplomatic coverage
* '''Sports''' (''Sports'') — O.League, OBSL, horse racing, combat sports, golf
* '''Editorial & Opinion''' (''Opinion'') — Editorials, columns, and commentary
* '''Regional''' (''Regional Briefs'') — Briefs from Okaiken's prefectures and municipalities

==Mercian Translation Edition==
{{See also|Toyozawa Mainichi/22 June 2026}}

Since 2023, the Toyozawa Mainichi has published a daily Mercian translation of its Sanese-language content. The Mercian edition is published digitally at en.toyozawa-mainichi.co.ok and as a limited broadsheet print run of approximately 10,000 copies.

Past editions of the Mercian edition are archived as wiki subpages at '''Toyozawa Mainichi/{date}'''.

